WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.799
OK, welcome, everyone.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.419
My name is Stephen Naron and I'm the

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.589
director of the Fortunoff Video

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.989
Archive for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.759
And it really is an honor for the

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.499
archive to be hosting this event

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.689
this morning to celebrate the

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.258
enormous scholarly contributions

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.088
of Professor Lawrence Langer and

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.939
welcome the publication of a series

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.469
of articles in his honor,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.269
in the honor of his storied career

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.620
and influence, entitled

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.150
"Interrogating Evil: A special

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.949
issue of the Journal of Holocaust

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.665
research for Lawrence Langer on his

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:31.950
90th birthday."

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.589
Larry is not only the most important

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:35.699
living thinker and writer on the

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.409
topic of Holocaust testimonies

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.239
from the Fortunoff Video Archives,

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.829
but he's also helped to build the

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:41.999
archive, having served as an

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.666
interviewer on over 80 testimonies

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.479
recorded by the archive since the

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.179
late 1980s. It's impossible

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.279
for me to overstate the

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.908
enormous influence Larry has had

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.969
on the shape of this archive and its

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.019
reception in the scholarly

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:55.979
community.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.629
But I suspect other speakers today

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:58.979
will address that much better than I

00:00:58.980 --> 00:00:59.939
can.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.319
I would like to make one tiny

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.549
announcement that I think is

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:03.689
relevant and I hope Larry will

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:04.690
appreciate.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:06.809
And that is, uh, I met recently with

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.369
representatives of Yale University

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.370
Press, and together

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.209
we are discussing the

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.219
creation of a multimedia version

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.749
of Larry's book, Holocaust

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.280
Testimonies: Ruins of Memory,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.229
which would combine the text of his

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.602
groundbreaking work with the

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.599
original excerpts from the

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.669
testimonies upon which Larry based

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:24.670
his research.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.459
In doing so, Ruins of Memory will

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.409
not only continue to tell us

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.029
about its own important insights

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.260
that can be drawn from testimony,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.005
but it will show us those important

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.150
insights.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.359
Larry, I hope to have it ready for

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.686
you before your 92nd

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.460
birthday, bis 120. So

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.709
now I'd like to hand things

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:44.837
over to Michal Aharony.

00:01:44.965 --> 00:01:46.950
Michal is the editor of the Journal

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.235
of Holocaust Research and

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.189
is a teaching fellow at Ben-Gurion

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.190
University.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.558
She received her Ph.D.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:53.909
in Political Science from the New

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.739
School for Social, Social Research

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.740
in 2010.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.319
Her research and teaching interests

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.180
include history of political ideas

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.759
in modern political thought and

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.800
Holocaust studies.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.263
She's the author of Hannah Arendt

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.385
and the Limits of Total Domination,

00:02:05.386 --> 00:02:07.229
The Holocaust Plurality

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.024
and Resistance from Routledge, 2015,

00:02:09.277 --> 00:02:11.339
and numerous articles

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.400
and chapters.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:13.750
Michal, you have the floor.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:19.490
Don't forget to unmute, Michal.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.430
Hi, can you hear me now?

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.029
Hi, so hello, everyone.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.536
Good morning. Here in Israel, it's

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.886
already 5:00 in the afternoon.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.849
I'm very happy to be here in this

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.159
special event to honor

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:41.370
Professor

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.560
Langer's 90th birthday and his

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:45.893
great contribution to Holocaust

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.160
studies.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.752
I want to say just

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.699
briefly a few words about

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.213
the journal for the sake of

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:57.015
people who don't know the

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.650
journal.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.023
So the Journal of Holocaust

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.380
Research, formerly known as Dapim,

00:03:03.461 --> 00:03:04.911
is the Academic Journal of the

00:03:04.912 --> 00:03:05.912
Weiss-Livnat International

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.989
Center for Holocaust Research

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.930
and Education at

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.619
the University of Haifa.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.646
It is published in, with

00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:15.934
coorporation

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.570
with Taylor and Francis

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:21.970
publications.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.589
And we publish

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.139
each year four volumes

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.730
in English and also

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.879
once a year a volume,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.259
a bigger volume in Hebrew.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:34.429
So it's bilingual.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.339
And on behalf of the journal,

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:38.595
I would like to take this

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.490
opportunity to thank,

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.909
first of all, Larry,

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.889
for giving us

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:46.970
the opportunity

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:49.417
to publish this special

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.759
volume in his honor

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.549
and also for his beautiful

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.470
article that he contributed

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:58.969
for this volume.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:00.990
Second,

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.979
I would like to thank the three

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:04.878
guest editors, Dawn Skorczewski, Dan

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:05.879
Stone, and Gabriel Finder.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:12.882
Thank you for your wonderful

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:15.037
cooperation and for all

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.744
the effort and hard work

00:04:17.765 --> 00:04:18.765
that

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.559
made this special

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.730
volume possible.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.899
For me personally, it was a

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.089
great privilege to work

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:29.929
with all of you.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:31.939
And of course, and

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.089
to read Larry's

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.180
piece and to work carefully on all

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:38.059
this...all the very

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.889
interesting and great

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.126
articles that all of you

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:42.810
contributed.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:44.550
And,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.869
we worked hard for over,

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.209
I think, one year

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:51.827
and a half. And I'm very happy to

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.300
be here tonight.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:55.850
And finally, I want to thank

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:58.943
the Fortunoff Video Archive

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.910
at Yale University for

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.307
organizing this special

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.410
event. So thank you, everyone,

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:08.748
and I hope everyone will enjoy this

00:05:08.749 --> 00:05:09.749
webinar.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.839
Thank you, Michal and thank you,

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.371
Stephen. I'd just like to say

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.769
a few words on behalf of the

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:18.770
editors. That's

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:21.140
Dawn and Gabi and myself.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.069
First of all, we'd like to

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.629
say thank you to the Fortunoff

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.369
Archive for hosting this event,

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.319
which, of course, would be nicer in

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.320
person. But

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:32.210
such,

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.219
such is life.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:35.837
We have to put up with, with this

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:37.010
instead.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.439
But maybe at some point we'll be

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.112
able to do something in, in

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.059
person. That's wonderful, Stephen,

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:43.516
to hear of the plans for, for

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:45.406
Larry's book. That's really, that's

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:46.910
really exciting.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:48.759
And secondly, to thank

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.434
Michal because what she said is, is

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.839
true. A lot, a lot of work has gone

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:53.840
into this journal.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:56.380
And we as editors,

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.059
I think, are extremely pleased with

00:05:58.060 --> 00:05:59.530
the results. In the end, we've

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:01.869
got a great range of

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.149
articles, very different sorts

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.040
of contributions, but all of them

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:08.079
reflecting on

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.959
and celebrating Larry's

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:12.283
work. And that's the points

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:14.470
we're really happy

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.269
to be able to present this

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.036
collection to, Larry, slightly

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:19.970
late for

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.910
his 90th birthday, only a year late,

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:24.629
but you know, never mind what's a

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:25.630
year? So

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:28.740
congratulations to Larry.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.839
And I'm now going to invite

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.879
each of the contributors to speak

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.169
very briefly for three or four

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.170
minutes each in

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.950
the order that they appear in

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.119
the journal.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.040
And then I'll invite Larry to

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.130
respond.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.130
And then if there are any questions

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.500
in the chat or in the Q&amp;A, then

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.620
I will read

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:49.919
out as many as we can in the time

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.750
that we have available to us.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.459
So I'd like first to ask

00:06:53.803 --> 00:06:55.499
Joanne Rudof to speak.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:56.781
Joanne is the Laurel Fox

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.100
Vlock fellow at the Fortunoff

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:00.180
Archive at Yale. Joanne.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:05.870
Oops, unmute

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:10.779
Joanne. There we go.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:12.330
Thank you Dan.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:13.359
Thank you Michal.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.459
And thank you, Stephen.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:16.914
Hello to the authors, most of whom I

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.038
know. And a special, special

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.070
hello to Larry.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.105
Hello also to everyone else who has

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.019
taken the time to be with us for

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.399
this wonderful occasion.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.070
Even if only virtually.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.745
And thank you to the Fortunoff Video

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:32.009
Archive for initiating and

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:33.180
hosting this event.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.119
When I was approached on April 5th,

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.279
2019, to write this piece

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.652
for the Journal, I was first

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.179
delighted to hear that my good

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.369
friend and mentor extraordinaire,

00:07:44.898 --> 00:07:46.564
Larry Langer, was to be honored on

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.959
the occasion of his 90th birthday

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:51.389
with a special issue of the journal.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:53.431
Second, I was very pleased to

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:54.990
be part of this effort.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:57.809
My journal article has a research

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.179
focus documenting Larry's career,

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.169
the history of the Fortunoff Video

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:03.785
Archive for Holocaust Testimonies,

00:08:03.786 --> 00:08:05.909
the connections

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.769
between Larry and Fortunoff since

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.137
1984, and the symbiosis

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:11.894
resulting in extraordinary

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.249
intellectual and research

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:15.340
impact since its

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.763
beginning and continuing to

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.490
the present and into the future.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.649
In contrast to the research focus

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:24.866
of the article, today I

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:27.370
will focus on the personal.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:29.866
Larry is extraordinarily

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:32.667
generous in sharing his

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.890
experience and his expertise.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:38.558
He is always willing to share his

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:40.629
phone number with anyone interested

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:42.070
in discussing his work,

00:08:42.549 --> 00:08:44.860
particularly with young scholars.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.230
He invites them to his home.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.379
He recommends to them what they

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:51.190
should be reading and viewing.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.369
And even more important, he listens

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.259
to their ideas and learns

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:57.260
from them.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.909
I have always considered myself

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:01.726
among one of the luckiest of those

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.330
working in our field

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.215
because of my mentors, my

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:06.820
extraordinary mentors.

00:09:06.825 --> 00:09:07.825
Geoffrey

00:09:14.224 --> 00:09:15.673
Hartman, Sam Kassow, Dana Kline,
Sandra Rosenstock, and Larry Langer.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.999
No course, no degree

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.129
program, no seminar

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.689
could begin to cover what I have

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:22.980
learned from all of them.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:26.269
In particular, Larry taught

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.120
me how to view video testimonies.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:32.360
When I began this work, although

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:34.639
each testimony had a huge impact

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:35.718
on me, I was really at

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:37.755
a loss in terms of seeing

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:40.579
patterns and critical

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:41.580
analysis.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:43.951
Larry's generosity in sharing

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.880
his ideas contributed greatly

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.159
to my own development as a scholar,

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.519
as an archivist, as a documentarian

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:53.640
and as a writer.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:56.820
I dare say there are countless

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:58.919
others who would say the very

00:09:58.920 --> 00:09:59.920
same thing.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.769
Larry's sense of humor,

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:04.490
his optimism, his

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:07.419
respect for others and his

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:08.912
ethics in terms of Holocaust

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:10.719
survivors and testimonies...but

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.619
equally important, his life,

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:17.349
combined with his tremendous

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:18.575
intellect, his insatiable

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:21.909
curiosity and impulse

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:24.400
to continually add to his knowledge,

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:26.135
his wit, his incomparable

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:28.130
writing,

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:31.149
all combined to make him truly

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:32.919
a giant in our field,

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.012
not only in the academy, but

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:37.180
in the world at large.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.169
His loving and close family are

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:41.840
the focus of his life.

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:44.642
My Zaide, my grandpa, would

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:46.100
call him a Mensch,

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.440
a Talmid Chocham, and perhaps even

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:53.429
an lamed vavnik. My Zaide, would

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:55.559
also have me say to Larry today,

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:01.499
gey gezunterheyt biz 120. Larry, I
wish

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.970
you many more Simhats and many

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.550
more great grandchildren,

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.655
particularly seeing the immense joy

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.392
that the babies Dana and Maya

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:10.810
have brought to you. Larry, L'chaim.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:15.909
Thank you so much,

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.039
Joanne. That's really a lovely way

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:19.040
to begin.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:21.669
Can I next ask

00:11:22.042 --> 00:11:23.042
Jennifer, Jennifer

00:11:23.947 --> 00:11:24.789
Geddes to speak.

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.919
Jennifer is a professor

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.269
in the Department of Religious

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:29.642
Studies at the University of

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:31.040
Virginia.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:34.429
Thank you. I am delighted to be here

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:35.606
today and really honored

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:38.059
to have been asked to contribute an

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.679
essay to this special issue

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:41.569
of the Journal of Holocaust

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:43.159
Research. So many thanks to the

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:44.875
editors, Dawn, Dan and Gabi for all

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:46.399
your work in bringing the issue into

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:47.400
being.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:49.013
And thanks to the Fortunoff Archive

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:50.539
for organizing this event today.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.399
Like all of us

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.017
here, I have learned so much from

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:55.638
reading Larry Langer's work,

00:11:56.199 --> 00:11:57.669
a learning that has influenced

00:11:58.070 --> 00:11:59.720
my writing and my teaching.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.089
I have seen the light of realization

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.019
on my students' faces as

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:06.549
they encountered his concept of

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.599
choiceless choice and its

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.189
implications for thinking about the

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:11.190
Holocaust.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:13.759
I have seen them begin to understand

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:15.554
their own temptations to domesticate

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:17.592
the Holocaust, after reading

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.035
his descriptions of the ways we try

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:22.249
to preempt, use, and abuse

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:23.377
the Holocaust. And they

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:25.789
have come to see the inadequacy of

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.229
language in grappling with the

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:28.215
Holocaust through his work.

00:12:28.216 --> 00:12:30.049
As Larry tells

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:32.239
us, quote, "the ghastly details

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.429
of the Holocaust are a constant

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:36.769
reminder of the abyss separating

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.387
the lived experience of those who

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:40.549
endured it from the language

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:41.863
that seeks to describe it," end

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:43.500
quote.

00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:45.482
In my essay, " Consider If: Levi,

00:12:45.483 --> 00:12:46.483
Langer, Agamben, and the
Unthinkable,"

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:51.135
I bring some of these things I have

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:52.699
learned from Langer's work into

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:53.863
conversation with Primo Levi's "If

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:56.685
this is a Man" and Giorgio Agamben's

00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:58.710
"Remnants of Auschwitz." Beginning

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:01.879
with Charlotte Delbo's distinction

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:03.469
between the worst and the

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:04.470
unthinkable, I try

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:07.519
to explore the unthinkable as

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:09.409
marking not the impossibility of

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:10.733
thought, but rather a space

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:13.519
that requires us to engage

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:15.410
in sustained reflection

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:17.379
that refuses clear judgments or

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:18.720
conclusions.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:21.088
I suggest that Levi's use of the

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:22.849
hypothetical, of "if" clauses,

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:25.828
create such a space and commands

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:27.942
us to engage in this kind of

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.160
reflection.

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:31.240
Specifically, I explore Levi's

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.409
command that we consider if the men

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.210
and women on the brink of exhaustion

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:36.769
and death in the concentration

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.869
camps, those who have been called

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:40.322
the Muselmänner, are human.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:43.759
I suggest that this activity of

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:45.619
"considering if" is one

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:47.840
that must not come to a conclusion

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:50.480
that is characterized by hesitation,

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:52.580
that requires a bracketing of

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.120
conclusion.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:57.229
I then connect this mode of ongoing

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:59.029
reflection in which judgment is

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.596
bracketed to Langer's

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:02.840
idea of choiceless choice,

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.119
suggesting that it, too, calls

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:06.889
for an ongoing attention and

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:08.556
engagement with the testimonies of

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.266
survivors, along with a bracketing

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:11.960
of moral judgment.

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:14.020
And finally, I turn to Agamben's

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:16.152
meditation on the Muselmann

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:18.475
in "Remnants of Auschwitz," arguing

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:20.449
that while the book seems to be

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:22.855
precisely this kind of reflection

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:25.190
without judgment or resolution,

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:27.925
Agamben actually fails to keep open

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.229
the space of reflection, conflating

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:31.579
victim and perpetrator in the

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.499
process. Where Levi

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:35.052
hesitates and Langer advises

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:36.720
caution, Agamben

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:39.019
advances conclusions.

00:14:40.010 --> 00:14:41.480
In the end, I suggest that the

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.489
unthinkable, while sometimes

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:45.049
functioning as a cliche,

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:47.479
can also mark a space in

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.100
which we are called to dwell,

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.511
listen and "consider if,"

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:54.620
a reflection that cannot conclude

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.749
or assert confident conclusions.

00:14:57.620 --> 00:14:58.894
I think this caution, this

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:01.187
willingness to continue to engage

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:03.529
the ghastly details that cannot

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:04.608
be fully understood is

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:07.309
something that Langer's work has

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:08.690
again and again taught me.

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:09.984
And I am very grateful and

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:11.580
Happy Birthday.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:15.580
Thank you, Jennifer.

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:17.490
That's really wonderful and also

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:19.349
lovely comments to hear.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.830
So our next speaker is

00:15:23.404 --> 00:15:24.972
Sara Horowitz who is a professor

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.149
at the Koschitzky Center for Jewish

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:29.490
Studies at York University.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:30.640
So, Sara.

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:35.460
Thank you. I'm going to echo

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:37.361
my colleagues in thanking the

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:39.960
editors of this volume

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.411
and of the journal itself for

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:43.779
bringing this into fruition.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.179
I know it's always difficult

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:47.909
to wrangle essays

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.225
into a cohesive volume as

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:50.830
they have done.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:52.835
But I do want to suggest that their

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.080
task was made a little bit lighter

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.870
by the inspiration that the honoree

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:00.610
has for all of

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:02.549
us contributors.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.279
And I want to begin by briefly

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:07.190
saying that in a field that's

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.915
been dominated, and perhaps rightly

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:10.112
so, by historians that

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.259
study the Holocaust, Larry Langer

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:14.260
is a luminary.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:15.269
He brought the tools and

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:16.495
methodologies of literary

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.959
scholarship to deepen

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:20.411
and enrich thinking about the

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:22.409
Holocaust. He's influenced people

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:23.831
in a range of discipline with

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:26.100
his rigorous, insightful,

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.199
and powerful voice.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.449
He has not only teased

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:32.339
out the subtleties and

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.349
nuances of writing

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:36.059
and testimonies about the Holocaust,

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:37.413
but he's also been a steady

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:39.962
critic of cultural misappropriations

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.450
of the Holocaust and

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.059
calling out as a kind of bad

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.084
faith the tendencies to

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:48.840
view the Shoah through

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:50.250
a redemptive lens.

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:51.659
His work has always been

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:53.179
characterized by clear thinking

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:55.979
and writing, by passion

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.409
and by a steady moral compass.

00:16:58.710 --> 00:17:00.779
I met Larry as a graduate student

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:02.880
in the late 80s when I asked him

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:04.709
on our first meeting whether

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:06.397
everything had already been said

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:07.949
about Holocaust literature.

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:09.352
And I think Larry's own work

00:17:10.109 --> 00:17:12.000
since the late 70s

00:17:12.569 --> 00:17:14.279
is certainly evidence that it had

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:16.259
not yet been already

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:17.260
said.

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:19.169
As I said, Larry has been a luminary

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.068
in the field for me.

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:21.069
His, he's been more.

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:23.250
He's been a generous mentor

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.160
and truly a lifelong

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:27.460
inspiration.

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:28.810
And so happy

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:31.650
birthday. And as we say in Yiddish,

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:34.289
tsu lange yor, a very long

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:35.290
life.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:37.289
I'm going to briefly summarize my

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:38.849
contribution to the volume,

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.219
which very much bears

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:42.328
the imprint of Larry's

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:44.760
insistence that we

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.098
not look for redemptive narratives

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:48.690
about the Holocaust.

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:51.589
I...my article is a reading of

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:53.550
the belated memoir

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:55.331
of the now "Rue Ordener, Rue Labat"

00:17:55.332 --> 00:17:56.749
by the French philosopher Sarah

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.039
Kofman, placed in the context

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:00.719
of fictional and nonfictional

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:02.092
accounts by writers who like

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:04.619
Kaufman, survived the Shoah

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:05.999
as children.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:07.882
Kofman was a child in Paris,

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:09.869
born to immigrant parents from

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:11.459
Poland, who Orthodox Jews.

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:12.803
Her father was the rabbi of

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:14.520
a Paris synagogue.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.529
He was tipped off in advance about

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:18.119
what came to be called the

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:20.140
Vel' d'Hiv rafle.

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:22.500
And after warning his congregants,

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:23.853
he returned home to his own

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:26.249
apartment and waited to be arrested,

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:28.529
hoping that in so doing he would buy

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:29.815
time for his wife and six

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:31.450
children.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:32.759
And indeed he did.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:34.439
He was murdered in Auschwitz but the

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.150
rest of his family survived.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:38.369
The memoir centers on a period of

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.529
time when Sarah, as a child,

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:42.158
and her mother lived out the war

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.280
sheltered by a French Catholic

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:45.889
neighbor who took them into her

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:48.119
apartment. More than 30 years

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:49.521
after the liberation of Paris,

00:18:49.535 --> 00:18:50.535
Kofman initiated

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:53.819
the process to have her rescuer

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:56.039
designated by Yad Vashem as one

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.810
of the righteous among the nations.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:18:59.999
But her memoir focuses

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:01.090
on what you

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:03.989
might call the dark side of being

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:04.990
hidden child.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:06.641
It would appear that Sarah is

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:08.765
very lucky. She never was separated

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:10.379
from her mother, and she lived out

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:12.750
the war, as did all of her siblings.

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:14.188
But the memoir focuses

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:16.210
on the way

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:20.310
her relationship with this woman

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:24.180
and the increasing powerlessness

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.280
of her own mother caused

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.130
a stress, a

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:29.519
break in the relationship of her

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:31.469
mother and a

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:32.894
kind of attenuation of her

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:35.010
relationship to Judaism as well.

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:36.862
I put this in the context of

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.390
writers like Appelfeld and

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:42.359
Louis Begley, where we can see

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:43.977
in the account of children during

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:46.019
the Holocaust a kind

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.020
of...anti-Bildungsroman.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:50.040
In other words, a kind of

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:52.370
undoing of the self, undoing

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:54.510
of the foundations of the self

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:56.730
so that who they become later

00:19:57.180 --> 00:19:59.399
is severely marked

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:01.170
by what they went through.

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:02.819
The memo was written in Sarah

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:04.290
Kofman's 60th year and shortly

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:05.769
before her suicide by

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.939
gas. And it functions, in a sense,

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.459
as a coda to her more abstract

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:13.260
and intellectual work published

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:14.517
several years earlier called

00:20:14.518 --> 00:20:15.518
"Paroles suffoquées",

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:17.480
or Smothered Words.

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:18.907
There she asks, how can

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:21.679
it be said and how can it not

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:23.386
be said? In her, in

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:25.475
her work, I very much...I'm

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.519
indebted to Larry's insistence

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:29.985
that we see not only what

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:32.569
it is that people say in

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:34.138
the writing, but also the things

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:36.200
that they cannot say.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:39.319
And often that they cannot bear to

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:40.643
think. So thank you, Larry,

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:43.459
for being my moral

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:44.460
compass.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:48.010
Thank you, Sara.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:50.130
More lovely comments.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:52.289
I see some common threads developing

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:53.700
here, which I'll

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:55.560
comment on a little later.

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:57.726
Our next speaker then is

00:20:58.415 --> 00:20:59.415
Noah Shenker.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.239
Noah is based at the Australian

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:05.099
Center for Jewish Civilization at

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:07.169
Monash University in Australia.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:08.969
And we're particularly grateful to

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:10.829
him for being here since it's about

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:11.910
two o'clock in the morning

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:13.989
where he is.

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:16.079
So Noah, if you're still awake, then

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:17.215
we'd love to hear from you.

00:21:17.216 --> 00:21:18.216
Though

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:20.339
I write about artificial

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:21.823
intelligence, I am currently

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:22.860
sentient.

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:25.440
I just want to start by saying

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:26.850
that though I've only

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:29.640
known Larry

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:31.165
for the last six or seven

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:33.303
years, I remember him first

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:35.700
meeting him at

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:38.759
AJS, the Association for Jewish

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:39.809
Studies, I believe it was in

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:40.810
Baltimore. And

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:43.390
I was just greatly

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:46.889
intimidated by the prospect of

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:48.507
meeting someone who had loomed so

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:50.850
large for me and I just recall

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:54.359
the warmth and his

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:56.100
generosity and

00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:57.920
his, his frank

00:21:58.310 --> 00:21:59.310
and, and, and

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:02.609
honest views

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:04.709
on everything, including my own

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:06.359
work, which were just incredibly

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:07.360
foundational

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:10.180
to helping me

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.780
pursue the path that I've been on.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.029
Though I first encountered Larry's

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.598
work and I remember exactly when

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:18.359
it was...I had just

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:21.259
decided upon a

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:23.309
topic for my dissertation

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:25.399
on the mediation of Holocaust's

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:27.796
testimony. And as a film and media

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:29.669
scholar, I

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:31.349
felt in over my head.

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:33.190
I had read a great

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:35.579
deal about the Holocaust.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:36.670
I had

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:39.509
done work

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:41.489
going back to my undergraduate

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:44.369
career, looking at representations

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:45.199
of the Holocaust.

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:46.670
But I just didn't have a sense

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:49.289
of how to enter into this discourse.

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:51.895
I needed a guide and that

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.090
guide ended up being

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:56.280
Larry. To, to read,

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:01.902
to read his work was to open

00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:03.650
up just

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:05.899
a whole world of possibilities for

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:06.900
how it was to understand

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:07.999
testimonies.

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:10.700
I had a sense

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:12.318
in terms of being a Film and media

00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:14.389
scholar about how the space of the

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:15.949
screen and the sound of the voice

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:18.109
and the ways in which people

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:19.984
perform and, and

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:23.070
articulate.

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:25.549
But I had no sense of

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:27.440
the kinds of conventions

00:23:27.470 --> 00:23:29.346
and the narrative and, and

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:31.920
rapport and all the, the

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:33.890
aspects that went into the process.

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:36.140
And when reading Larry's

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:38.509
formative work, it

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:40.080
opened up for

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:42.920
me what ended up being

00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:46.250
the passion that drives my,

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:47.830
my, my career.

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:49.249
And I'm just forever indebted

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:51.589
to Larry for that.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:52.849
And I'm indebted to the

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:56.299
editors for inviting me, for

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:57.587
giving me this ability,

00:23:58.550 --> 00:23:59.839
to feel honored, to be able to

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:01.220
contribute to this volume.

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:04.309
I'm a little emotional just because

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:05.479
it takes me back to first

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:06.791
encountering Larry's work and onto

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:08.191
his, ongoing genorosity and warmth

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:10.969
that he's... an honest,

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:13.279
I can, I cannot stress honesty and

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:15.140
frankness when he has talked

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:17.090
and we've talked about our ideas.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:19.880
My work for this volume disrupted

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:21.000
narratives. Lawrence Langer's
explorations

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:24.049
of deep memory and Holocaust

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:26.059
testimonies is

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:28.948
a way of trying to trace Larry's

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:31.489
influence, the ways

00:24:31.490 --> 00:24:33.156
in which he's been so foundational

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:34.738
and foregrounding the importance

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:36.607
of examining Holocaust testimonies,

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:38.240
giving us the language,

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:39.660
giving us

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.859
the sense of the conventions,

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:44.135
which is probably not the best term,

00:24:44.136 --> 00:24:45.500
but at least a sense

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:48.259
of the textures

00:24:49.310 --> 00:24:50.310
and

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:52.414
the contours of testimonies.

00:24:52.415 --> 00:24:53.780
And also

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:56.689
really allowing

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:59.209
historians and other scholars of the

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:00.890
Holocaust to look at testimonies,

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:02.719
not just simply as primary

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:04.430
documents, as primary sources, but

00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:05.460
as...of the

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:09.430
representations about their

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:11.559
experiences, but as other sources in

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:12.560
and of themselves. In

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:15.999
short, I think, Larry, more than any

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:17.919
other scholar, has demonstrated

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:20.410
the interwoven nature of history

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:22.269
and of memory as it

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:24.866
makes its way in video testimonies,

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:28.509
whether it's...and I've continued

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:29.829
to...whether it's the students that

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:31.104
I supervise and constantly

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:33.819
return in the classes that I teach

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:35.448
to, to Larry's conceptualization

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:36.920
of the choiceless choice.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:39.113
And what my work focuses in

00:25:39.640 --> 00:25:41.589
on is particularly his

00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:43.077
engagement with Charlotte Delbo's

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:45.849
notions of deep and common

00:25:45.850 --> 00:25:48.219
memory, and the ways

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:49.310
in which

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:52.239
survivors express common

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:53.808
memory, often in a chronological

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:55.960
or coherent structure,

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:58.175
recalling in the present moment how

00:25:58.390 --> 00:25:59.739
events unfolded in a

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:01.210
compartmentalized past and in,

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:02.584
in contrast, notions

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:06.899
of deep memory, taking witnesses

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:08.890
back to the events, reintroducing

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:09.970
them to the range of senses

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:11.613
experienced at the time and

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:13.619
complicating. And this is so, so

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.089
foundational, complicating any

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:16.470
efforts to keep that past

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:18.460
at bay.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:19.959
And that, Larry, more

00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:22.082
than any other scholar that I know,

00:26:22.083 --> 00:26:23.083
faced...compelling

00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:27.559
us to, to engage the paradox

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:29.359
that it is impossible to fully

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:31.240
understand a witness,

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:32.750
but that we must always try to

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:34.229
understand a witness.

00:26:34.558 --> 00:26:36.950
That the impossibility

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:38.744
and the obligation that must coexist

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:41.089
in this endeavor has

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:43.700
forever shaped our understanding

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:45.500
of Holocaust testimony.

00:26:45.530 --> 00:26:46.544
And I think also of, of, of, of

00:26:46.545 --> 00:26:47.545
trauma

00:26:48.530 --> 00:26:49.759
more broadly. And I think

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:52.430
of genocides beyond the Holocaust

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:53.640
by way of conclusion.

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:54.670
And as I'm speaking

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:57.599
from Australia and starting to

00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:59.364
do work on the Stolen Generations of

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:01.279
Australia and as I've done work

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:03.259
on Cambodia and teach on Rwanda

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:04.880
and other genocides, to see

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:07.430
Larry's conceptualizations

00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:08.775
and also his critiques of

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:10.880
other ways of conducting

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:13.039
testimonies that have been done by

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:15.573
various archives across the world,

00:27:15.574 --> 00:27:17.599
to see how the debates

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:18.600
that Larry

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:20.599
had mentioned in regards to the

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:22.969
Holocaust continue to inform

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:24.859
in critical ways how

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:26.450
we understand genocide

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:28.280
and testimony, well beyond the

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:29.700
Holocaust.

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:32.329
So, Larry, thank you again

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:34.479
for informing all of this work,

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:36.799
for steering all of this work and

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:38.417
again to the editors for bringing

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:39.990
his work in front

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:43.659
and center where it belongs and as

00:27:43.660 --> 00:27:44.660
it always has been.

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:47.659
Thank you Noah. Thank you for your

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:49.849
powerful comments.

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:53.869
Our next speaker is

00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:54.980
Dawn Skorczewski.

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:57.289
Dawn is Professor

00:27:57.290 --> 00:27:59.359
of Literature at Brandeis University

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:01.279
and she's going to talk

00:28:01.490 --> 00:28:03.380
about the article, which

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:05.976
she and I have co-written for this

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:07.880
special issue, which is on

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:09.785
Jan Karski's testimonies.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:11.240
Dawn, go on.

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:13.609
I'll talk a little bit about the

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:14.929
article. But first, I just want to

00:28:14.930 --> 00:28:16.215
say a round of thank yous.

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:18.352
W.S. Merwin's poem "Thank

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:19.939
you" comes to mind.

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:20.959
I don't know if you know it, but

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:21.871
it's just thank you, thank you,

00:28:21.872 --> 00:28:22.909
thank you, thank you.

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:25.430
The first thank you has to go to

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:26.460
Lawrence Langer. Larry, I

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:30.079
am filled with emotion as I think

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.099
about the many years now.

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:33.589
I mean, almost a decade.

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:35.419
But for me it seems like a very long

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:36.799
time that I've been working with you

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:38.466
and talking to you about Holocaust

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:40.534
testimony, testimonies.

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:43.317
A couple of years ago when you were

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:45.114
about to turn 90, I remember talking

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:46.449
with Dan and thinking...how could,

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:48.499
how could we, how could I ever

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:49.500
repay you for all

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:51.829
that I've learned from you and for

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:53.150
all you've given to the field?

00:28:53.380 --> 00:28:54.850
And this volume, which I've so

00:28:55.190 --> 00:28:56.780
enjoyed working on with Dan and Gabi

00:28:56.781 --> 00:28:58.819
has been a labor

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:01.490
of love, as well as an intellectual,

00:29:01.690 --> 00:29:03.160
a very gratifying intellectual

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:04.500
experience.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:06.229
I first met Larry when I was giving

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:08.089
a talk about Anne

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:09.090
Frank and Etty

00:29:09.928 --> 00:29:11.240
Hillesum at the Newton Public Library

00:29:11.390 --> 00:29:12.500
in Newton, Massachusetts.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:14.119
And, and he was in the audience

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:16.460
raising his hand every so often,

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:18.679
correcting the speakers about

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:19.856
some errors in, in their

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:21.889
delivery, things that they were

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:23.630
saying. I was one of those speakers

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:24.979
and I was the one who was getting

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:26.299
the most corrections, I think.

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:27.769
And at the end of the talk, Larry

00:29:27.770 --> 00:29:29.387
came to the front of the room and

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:31.519
he said to me, you know, there are a

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:33.019
few things I didn't get to say that

00:29:33.020 --> 00:29:34.609
you missed in your talk, that you're

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:35.960
just, you're just wrong about.

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:37.459
And I was thinking, oh my goodness.

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:39.109
I was so taken aback.

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:40.874
And I knew there were probably a lot

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:42.079
of things, but I didn't know who

00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:43.670
this person was and I didn't know

00:29:44.510 --> 00:29:45.709
what I was going to...how I was

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:47.029
going to respond because the talk

00:29:47.030 --> 00:29:47.899
was over.

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:49.219
And I said, well you must know a lot

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:50.059
about this.

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:51.019
And he said, well, you know, I've

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:52.450
written 11 books on the subject.

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:54.019
And I said, oh, maybe I should know

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:55.009
your name. He said, I'm Lawrence

00:29:55.010 --> 00:29:56.630
Langer. And I almost fainted.

00:29:57.170 --> 00:29:59.029
And I was standing in front of

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:00.110
Lawrence Langer. And then, almost

00:30:00.650 --> 00:30:02.299
the next thing that happened was he

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.099
graciously invited me to come to his

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:05.779
house and talk more about Holocaust

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:07.020
testimonies.

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:08.659
And we started watching Holocaust

00:30:08.660 --> 00:30:10.729
testimonies in his living

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:12.739
room, pretty much once a week.

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:15.390
So I've spent, I don't know, 100,

00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:17.275
over 100 hours watching testimonies

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:19.190
with Larry and talking about

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:21.430
so many different aspects of them.

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.479
And so you can imagine just from

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:25.408
those words how much...what that

00:30:25.550 --> 00:30:26.689
experience could be like.

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:28.640
And then to have the opportunity now

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:30.301
to work on this volume in his

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:32.239
honor and to read the beautiful

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:33.829
essays that you've all written and

00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:35.599
to see Larry's face as he listens to

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:36.949
you. It's just such a pleasure.

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:38.390
I'm not going to talk a lot about

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:40.097
the article that Dan and I wrote,

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:42.259
except to say that Dan and I met

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:43.699
a few years ago. And one of our

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:45.529
first conversations was about Larry

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:47.359
Langer, about his work and

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:49.190
also about his interview with

00:30:49.430 --> 00:30:50.430
Jan Karski.

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:52.579
I'm just going to show you a picture

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.148
that appears in our article, and

00:30:54.710 --> 00:30:56.369
I'm sharing my screen here.

00:30:56.690 --> 00:30:58.759
It's a picture of Karski and

00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:00.710
he's imitating FDR.

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:02.480
And you'll see him right here.

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.609
And Dan and I were talking

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:05.959
about this. And I remember Larry

00:31:05.960 --> 00:31:07.002
telling me about this, this,

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:08.774
this happening in the interview.

00:31:08.775 --> 00:31:10.143
And he said, you have to see this,

00:31:10.144 --> 00:31:11.809
this is his Shoah foundation

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:13.819
interview. And then Dan

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:15.437
and I started comparing the Shoah

00:31:15.650 --> 00:31:17.267
foundation interview with Larry's

00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:19.609
Fortunoff interview with Karski and

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:21.198
then with Claude Lanzmann's Shoah.

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:23.900
And it was such a rich conversation.

00:31:23.930 --> 00:31:25.759
And the result of which is in this

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:27.328
article. We conclude the article

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:29.839
with this comment, which

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:30.840
I think is so...it attempts

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:34.099
to capture what Larry

00:31:34.100 --> 00:31:35.929
is about as a thinker and scholar.

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:37.849
Thirty years later, Langer's work

00:31:37.850 --> 00:31:39.799
helps us to correct a tendency

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:42.200
to focus exclusively

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:44.329
on the traumatic, the historical,

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:46.309
or the performative dimensions

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:47.690
of our recorded testimony.

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:49.569
Instead, we must keep all these

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:51.399
theories handy and at bay as we

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:53.329
listen from moment to moment across

00:31:53.330 --> 00:31:55.309
the archives to the individual human

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:57.019
being who endured great suffering

00:31:57.080 --> 00:31:58.256
and acted heroically and

00:31:58.970 --> 00:31:59.970
in Lanzmann's, and in Karski's case,
who

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:03.009
insisted I can't go back in his

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.125
interview with Lanzmann, only to do

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:06.612
so again and again for these

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:07.880
important archives.

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:09.919
It was such a pleasure to work on

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:10.989
this article with Dan.

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:12.764
It went through a few iterations and

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:14.059
one of the most important parts of

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:15.499
that was talking to Larry about the

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:16.500
article.

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:17.989
There's so much more I'd like to say

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:20.509
as far as what a pleasure it's been

00:32:20.510 --> 00:32:21.859
to work on this volume and to work

00:32:21.860 --> 00:32:23.059
with Larry. But I'm not going to

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:24.079
take up all of our time.

00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:25.219
And I want to thank you all for

00:32:25.220 --> 00:32:26.599
being here. And thank you, Stephen,

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:27.600
for hosting.

00:32:27.650 --> 00:32:29.180
And thank you to the Journal.

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:30.469
And again, thanks to all the

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:31.819
contributors. And finally, thanks

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:32.900
again to Larry.

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:36.240
Thanks so much, Dawn.

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:38.089
Well, just quickly to say that it

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:39.499
was a pleasure to work with you,

00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:41.119
too, on on that piece, and

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:44.509
it's, it's really an honor

00:32:44.730 --> 00:32:45.955
to, to have written it in

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:47.750
Larry's honor. So

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:51.140
the next speaker would have been

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:52.621
Barry Langford. But he is not

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:54.529
here. So I will just, as his

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:55.774
colleague and friend at Royal

00:32:55.775 --> 00:32:56.809
Holloway I will just advertise his

00:32:56.810 --> 00:32:58.670
article and which is

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:00.500
on film,

00:33:00.680 --> 00:33:02.059
American Films and The

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:03.419
Americanization of

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:04.950
the Holocaust.

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:06.650
And it is a brilliant piece which

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:08.975
shows, as has already been said,

00:33:08.976 --> 00:33:10.400
how, how Larry's

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:12.529
ideas, in this case about the

00:33:12.530 --> 00:33:13.611
Americanization of the Holocaust,

00:33:13.612 --> 00:33:15.489
were in many ways

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:16.910
way ahead of their time.

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:18.979
How in some ways they have been

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:21.469
criticized by more recent

00:33:21.740 --> 00:33:22.519
scholarship.

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:24.049
But he returns the argument full

00:33:24.050 --> 00:33:25.999
circle and goes back to

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:27.380
Larry's ideas and shows why

00:33:27.890 --> 00:33:29.240
they're still important for

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:32.119
continuing to understand American

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:33.919
films about the Holocaust.

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:35.900
So it's really a wonderful piece.

00:33:36.170 --> 00:33:37.609
So our final contributor is

00:33:38.390 --> 00:33:39.769
Gabi Finder, who's also,

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:41.900
as you know, one of the co editors

00:33:42.370 --> 00:33:43.730
and Gabi in the Department of

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:45.440
Germanic Languages and Literature

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:48.170
at the University of Virginia.

00:33:48.290 --> 00:33:49.290
So, Gabi.

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:50.980
Thank you very much Dan.

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:53.460
I, too, have many thanks.

00:33:53.820 --> 00:33:55.319
I don't know, I didn't know the poem

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:57.020
by Merwin, but Dawn, please

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:59.099
send it to me after the webinar.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:00.690
I loved what you said.

