00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:39.809
been looking for to write about this

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:41.489
particular piece.

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:43.960
Thank you, Stephen who

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:47.039
I think is...not I think, I know is

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:48.210
a new friend of mine.

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:50.149
Thank you, Stephen, for hosting

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:51.501
this and being a friend.

00:35:51.502 --> 00:35:53.249
I want to thank the Fortunoff

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:54.280
Archives.

00:35:56.260 --> 00:35:57.583
Thank you, Joanne, for your

00:35:57.670 --> 00:35:59.679
wonderful tribute to Larry in your

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:01.149
introductory essay.

00:36:01.180 --> 00:36:03.460
It really brings Larry's life

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:05.030
to life.

00:36:05.350 --> 00:36:07.149
You know, instead of...we write

00:36:07.270 --> 00:36:08.887
a bit in the abstract, and Joanne

00:36:09.100 --> 00:36:11.020
brought Larry to life.

00:36:12.130 --> 00:36:14.409
Finally, I must thank Professor

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:15.410
Lawrence Langer.

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:19.090
He's meant a lot to me in my career.

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:20.679
He doesn't know that, but he has

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:22.659
meant a lot to me in my career.

00:36:23.350 --> 00:36:24.679
But I also want to talk about...I

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:26.529
want to mention a personal story

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:27.939
that is very important to me, just

00:36:27.940 --> 00:36:29.409
briefly in my career.

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:31.539
Those of you who know me and know my

00:36:31.540 --> 00:36:32.499
work, know that I've written a lot

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:33.879
about Jewish honor courts,

00:36:34.780 --> 00:36:35.979
courts that Jewish communities

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:37.510
established after the Holocaust

00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:39.699
to investigate fellow Jews accused

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:40.750
of having dirty hands.

00:36:40.780 --> 00:36:41.780
And Larry's

00:36:43.480 --> 00:36:45.699
notion or concept of choiceless

00:36:45.700 --> 00:36:47.530
choices meant a lot to me

00:36:48.220 --> 00:36:50.320
as I have worked on this topic for

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.239
years now and continue to

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:53.240
work on it.

00:36:54.010 --> 00:36:56.320
In general, Larry's

00:36:56.680 --> 00:36:57.807
empathic and forthright

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:00.789
approach to the Holocaust, to

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:01.917
Holocaust testimony has

00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:04.376
been an inspiration to me and I've

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:05.760
tried to emulate him.

00:37:05.800 --> 00:37:07.479
But I have a special personal story

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:09.048
that I have to share with all of

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:11.139
you, because it's been important to

00:37:11.140 --> 00:37:12.267
me and to my family and to my

00:37:13.570 --> 00:37:15.039
life, really.

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:16.479
And Larry knows what I'm talking

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:18.069
about and Dan knows what I'm talking

00:37:18.070 --> 00:37:19.809
about. Joanne knows what I'm talking

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:20.810
about.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:23.850
So my father is with us today.

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:25.809
My father and Larry are two months

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:27.279
apart. I believe that my father is

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:28.900
exactly two months older

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:31.499
than Larry, both for 91.

00:37:32.590 --> 00:37:34.011
My father, Herbert Finder, is

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:36.489
with us today. My dad is a Holocaust

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:38.058
survivor. My mother also and her

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:39.940
family left Germany after

00:37:40.150 --> 00:37:41.290
the night of Broken Glass.

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:42.496
But my dad with, with my

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:44.049
grandfather, went through several

00:37:44.050 --> 00:37:45.180
camps as a

00:37:45.910 --> 00:37:47.439
youn, as a boy, really.

00:37:47.980 --> 00:37:50.139
And for years, I tried

00:37:50.140 --> 00:37:51.670
to persuade my dad

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:54.099
to give testimony,

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:56.106
audio testimony.

00:37:56.107 --> 00:37:57.810
He'd once gave in the 1980s, he

00:37:58.540 --> 00:37:59.540
gave testimony

00:38:00.670 --> 00:38:02.410
on a cassette tape

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:03.700
to Nora Levin.

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:05.679
And that actually... one can find

00:38:05.680 --> 00:38:06.969
that on the website of the United

00:38:06.970 --> 00:38:08.439
States Holocaust Memorial Museum.

00:38:08.860 --> 00:38:10.624
But, you know, I wanted my dad to do

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:12.249
what other survivors

00:38:12.700 --> 00:38:13.700
have done. So, you know, so

00:38:14.290 --> 00:38:15.564
important to give out your

00:38:15.963 --> 00:38:17.530
testimony. I couldn't persuade him,

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:18.759
even though he had such an

00:38:18.760 --> 00:38:19.780
interesting story,

00:38:20.620 --> 00:38:22.510
because he always felt that

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:25.059
younger people who would interview

00:38:25.060 --> 00:38:26.979
him, wouldn't really understand

00:38:27.170 --> 00:38:28.629
him, couldn't understand what he

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:30.150
went through. So he once said, if he

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:31.749
could find...if I could persuade

00:38:31.750 --> 00:38:33.610
him, find someone who would

00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:35.610
understand his story,

00:38:35.650 --> 00:38:36.549
he would do it.

00:38:36.550 --> 00:38:38.170
Well, I thought of Lawrence Langer,

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:39.519
but I didn't really know

00:38:40.180 --> 00:38:41.019
Larry.

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:42.277
And then I was at an, maybe the same

00:38:43.417 --> 00:38:45.159
AJS conference in Baltimore, to

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:46.280
which Noah referred earlier, and

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:48.249
after...no one was even on that

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:49.622
panel, I believe, if I'm not

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:52.139
mistaken. Joanne was on the panel.

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:53.919
My good friend Avinoam Patt was on

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:54.920
the panel.

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:57.019
And afterwards, I was scared to

00:38:57.070 --> 00:38:58.344
death, intimidated because

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:01.109
who am I? I'm just a minor scholar

00:39:01.120 --> 00:39:03.280
and Larry is such a major scholar.

00:39:03.310 --> 00:39:04.310
He was on the panel.

00:39:04.780 --> 00:39:06.669
But anyway, I went afterwards and

00:39:06.670 --> 00:39:08.679
I said...we had met once

00:39:08.680 --> 00:39:10.052
before, years ago, he didn't

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:12.399
remember...I said, Professor Langer,

00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:13.998
you know, I admire your work so much

00:39:13.999 --> 00:39:16.249
and my dad is a Holocaust survivor

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:17.349
and he's probably more or less your

00:39:17.350 --> 00:39:18.429
age, and he always said that he

00:39:18.610 --> 00:39:20.109
wouldn't give testimony, he has such

00:39:20.110 --> 00:39:21.629
an important story to tell, but

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:23.619
he won't do it because he feels that

00:39:23.620 --> 00:39:25.119
younger people wouldn't understand

00:39:25.120 --> 00:39:26.639
him, and he said that you would

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:28.684
understand him, would you mind doing

00:39:28.690 --> 00:39:30.219
this? And this was about five or six

00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:32.139
years ago. Larry was retired and

00:39:32.140 --> 00:39:33.039
lives in Boston.

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:34.529
The Fortunoff Archive, of course, is

00:39:34.530 --> 00:39:35.530
in New Haven.

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:36.810
I said, would you do it? And he said,

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:38.044
without blinking, and I said yes.

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:41.043
And it was about five years

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:43.499
ago in the summer, I drove my dad

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:44.578
from New Jersey to New

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:46.289
Haven.

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:47.956
And Joanne was very gracious to be

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:49.060
there, too.

00:39:49.110 --> 00:39:50.761
And Joanne

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:53.559
and Larry interviewed my dad.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:55.949
And I just want to say...it

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.079
was a couple of hours. Stephen was

00:39:58.080 --> 00:39:59.610
there in the control room.

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:00.644
That's where I first met Stephen.

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:02.380
And I just want to say that, that

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:04.322
testimony, I think it's...you know,

00:40:04.323 --> 00:40:05.323
it's important.

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:08.408
But to my family and to me, it's

00:40:08.820 --> 00:40:09.820
a gift.

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:12.989
You know, because it's something

00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:15.119
that I will always

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:16.210
have. And my children,

00:40:17.370 --> 00:40:18.493
that's what really important to me,

00:40:18.494 --> 00:40:20.219
it's my children have it.

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:21.433
And I will never forget....how

00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:25.944
my children, how they...when

00:40:26.460 --> 00:40:27.979
they saw that testimony for the

00:40:28.020 --> 00:40:29.879
first time, what an effect

00:40:30.120 --> 00:40:31.289
it had on them. And they saw it

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:33.429
together with my dad and

00:40:33.430 --> 00:40:34.866
I...I just, it

00:40:35.510 --> 00:40:37.349
is etched in my mind

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:39.179
forever. My daughter, or my

00:40:39.180 --> 00:40:41.039
child, even wants to write a

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:43.319
music to it.

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:44.839
So it's just, it's a gift to me

00:40:45.150 --> 00:40:46.199
and my family.

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:48.030
And I just...Larry, you've given me

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:49.889
and my family a great gift,

00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:52.440
and I can never thank you

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:54.179
enough for it.

00:40:57.310 --> 00:40:58.682
So I write about...my article

00:40:59.980 --> 00:41:01.089
is called...I'll speak about a very

00:41:01.090 --> 00:41:02.707
briefly, and my article is called

00:41:03.090 --> 00:41:04.419
"A Horrific Impression of Jewish

00:41:04.420 --> 00:41:06.086
Martyrdom: Regarding Extermination

00:41:06.340 --> 00:41:08.739
of Polish Jews: Album of Pictures."

00:41:09.370 --> 00:41:10.644
And I write about an album

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:12.669
of photographs

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:15.219
called...it was a multilingual

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:17.439
folder, a volume of photographs

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:18.440
from the Holocaust...in Polish it's

00:41:22.647 --> 00:41:23.823
Zaglada Zydostwa Polskiego: Album
Zdjec. It's translated

00:41:23.824 --> 00:41:25.062
as Extermination of Polish Jews

00:41:25.063 --> 00:41:26.889
Album of Pictures.

00:41:27.250 --> 00:41:29.289
It was published in 1946

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:31.359
by the Jewish, Central Jewish

00:41:31.360 --> 00:41:33.850
Historical Commission in Poland.

00:41:36.160 --> 00:41:37.777
To the best of my knowledge, this

00:41:38.020 --> 00:41:39.909
is the first album of its

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:42.039
kind published by a Jewish

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:43.944
institution after the Holocaust,

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:46.780
that is Holocaust of photographs.

00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:49.719
I'm not...everybody who knows me

00:41:49.720 --> 00:41:51.249
knows that I'm a dinosaur when it

00:41:51.250 --> 00:41:52.119
comes to these things.

00:41:52.120 --> 00:41:53.259
But I'm going to try to share my

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:54.260
screen.

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:56.877
Can you...whoops, wrong thing.

00:41:58.210 --> 00:41:59.469
Let's see, my son is here.

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:00.760
Maybe he can help me.

00:42:01.030 --> 00:42:02.030
Hello, can you...this? Yes.

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:10.729
Oh, there it is. Can you see that?

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:12.139
Does everybody see that?

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:13.310
So that's the album

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:16.090
that I used

00:42:16.100 --> 00:42:17.359
and it's gone, as you see.

00:42:17.360 --> 00:42:19.309
And now...can you bring me back

00:42:19.310 --> 00:42:20.310
to Stop Share?

00:42:21.640 --> 00:42:22.609
OK. So there I am.

00:42:22.610 --> 00:42:23.610
Thank you.

00:42:23.750 --> 00:42:25.099
It's good to have a young person

00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:26.719
with me to help me with me these

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:27.529
things.

00:42:27.530 --> 00:42:28.730
My son is here helping me.

00:42:29.150 --> 00:42:31.369
So this was...this album

00:42:31.400 --> 00:42:32.809
is, to the best of my knowledge, the

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:34.610
first album of its kind.

00:42:35.510 --> 00:42:36.510
And it shows Holocaust...photographs

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:38.554
from the Holocaust in the, within

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:41.179
a one year after the end of World

00:42:41.180 --> 00:42:42.530
War Two and the Holocaust, the

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:44.119
Central Jewish Historical Commission

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:46.159
in Poland had collected some

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:48.020
3000 photographs

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:49.639
from the Holocaust.

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:52.520
And the interesting thing

00:42:52.550 --> 00:42:54.050
or the catch is that

00:42:54.410 --> 00:42:55.831
most of the photos were taken

00:42:56.420 --> 00:42:57.770
by Germans.

00:42:58.600 --> 00:42:59.600
How they came into

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:02.419
the possession of Jews after the war

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:04.340
is an interesting story in itself.

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:07.879
Many Germans took...they

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:09.851
took their lightweight cameras.

00:43:10.670 --> 00:43:12.559
They took photographs of

00:43:13.160 --> 00:43:15.349
every aspect of the Holocaust,

00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:17.739
you know, to mock the Jews, to

00:43:17.780 --> 00:43:18.780
torment them.

00:43:18.890 --> 00:43:20.659
They showed themselves having a

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:22.070
great grand old time

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:24.500
tormenting and killing Jews.

00:43:25.760 --> 00:43:27.889
They had those art photographs

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:30.620
developed by Polish developers,

00:43:30.650 --> 00:43:32.110
even some Jewish developers in

00:43:32.210 --> 00:43:33.919
ghettos. They replicated them.

00:43:33.930 --> 00:43:35.150
They gave them to friends.

00:43:35.180 --> 00:43:37.190
And they were circulating throughout

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:39.349
Nazi occupied Poland and other

00:43:39.350 --> 00:43:40.350
places.

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:41.827
And both Poles and Jews

00:43:42.800 --> 00:43:44.269
came into possession of many of

00:43:44.270 --> 00:43:46.129
those photographs, both during and

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:47.160
after World War

00:43:48.110 --> 00:43:49.188
Two and the Holocaust.

00:43:49.195 --> 00:43:51.500
The album,

00:43:51.910 --> 00:43:53.929
when a majority of the photographs

00:43:53.960 --> 00:43:55.479
in the archives, photo archives

00:43:56.060 --> 00:43:57.499
of the Central Jewish Historical

00:43:57.500 --> 00:43:59.019
Commission were German produced

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:00.319
photographs.

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:01.729
There were also some that were taken

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:03.250
by Jews and even by Poles.

00:44:03.290 --> 00:44:05.390
But the majority of the photographs

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:06.940
had been taken by Germans.

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:08.780
And the album, which

00:44:09.080 --> 00:44:11.329
contains some 250 photographs,

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:12.934
the majority of the photos

00:44:13.550 --> 00:44:14.726
in that album are German

00:44:15.560 --> 00:44:17.689
produced photographs.

00:44:18.620 --> 00:44:20.360
And I...in my

00:44:21.560 --> 00:44:23.840
article, I discussed the dilemma

00:44:24.440 --> 00:44:25.440
that the Jewish

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:28.489
Historical Commission and the editor

00:44:28.940 --> 00:44:30.829
of this album

00:44:31.120 --> 00:44:33.050
faced, and that was they

00:44:33.170 --> 00:44:34.789
had...they wanted to

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:37.364
produce an album of photographs from

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:38.989
the Holocaust. They wanted to

00:44:41.060 --> 00:44:43.489
evoke moral outrage

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:45.739
among an international community.

00:44:45.770 --> 00:44:47.599
But they were stuck with a

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:48.727
majority of photographs

00:44:49.550 --> 00:44:51.020
from the German perspective of

00:44:51.440 --> 00:44:52.440
what

00:44:54.350 --> 00:44:56.130
scholars called the German gaze.

00:44:56.600 --> 00:44:58.459
And I make the

00:44:58.460 --> 00:44:59.809
argument in my

00:45:01.190 --> 00:45:02.680
article that the editor...could

00:45:03.740 --> 00:45:04.740
you help me again for a second again?

00:45:06.380 --> 00:45:07.388
So I want to just show you the...this
one. Which

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:17.988
one is it? This one.

00:45:17.989 --> 00:45:18.989
Oh, hold on.

00:45:28.910 --> 00:45:30.089
Oh, there it is. Can you all see

00:45:30.090 --> 00:45:31.049
that?

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:32.639
It's good to have a 20 year old son

00:45:32.640 --> 00:45:33.869
in college who can help with these

00:45:33.870 --> 00:45:34.870
things.

00:45:34.930 --> 00:45:36.008
And this is the editor

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:37.829
of the album

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:41.039
Gershon Taffet, who himself was a

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:43.530
Holocaust survivor, who survived

00:45:44.580 --> 00:45:46.070
the liquidation of his ghetto

00:45:47.271 --> 00:45:48.142
Zólkiew, who also survived the

00:45:48.143 --> 00:45:49.760
Janowska, the infamous, notorious

00:45:50.005 --> 00:45:52.050
Janowska labor camp outside

00:45:53.850 --> 00:45:55.049
Lvov/L'viv, who lost his wife and

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:56.459
daughter. And then after

00:45:57.030 --> 00:45:58.559
the Holocaust, joined the Central

00:45:58.560 --> 00:46:00.239
Jewish Historical Commission and

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:01.808
became the director of its photo

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:03.839
archive or one of the directors and

00:46:03.840 --> 00:46:05.909
the editor of this

00:46:06.750 --> 00:46:07.750
volume.

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:09.679
So I'm back. So I argued

00:46:09.680 --> 00:46:11.189
that even with

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:13.317
the inherent limitations of these

00:46:13.590 --> 00:46:15.570
photos. Taffet and his colleagues

00:46:15.590 --> 00:46:16.890
on the Jewish Historical Commission

00:46:17.130 --> 00:46:18.257
decided to produce this

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:20.736
volume because they wanted...their

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:22.767
aim was to bring an international

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:24.400
mass audience closer

00:46:25.230 --> 00:46:26.504
to the horrors of the Nazi

00:46:26.730 --> 00:46:28.500
persecution and

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:29.940
murder of Jews.

00:46:31.030 --> 00:46:32.157
And I also a show a few

00:46:33.150 --> 00:46:34.619
of these photographs in my

00:46:34.660 --> 00:46:36.599
album...in my article.

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:38.324
I don't want to, I decided

00:46:39.090 --> 00:46:40.707
not to show the most horrific and

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:42.570
the worst of the photographs.

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:43.919
But I do want to show...I did show

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:46.050
one so-called buddy picture.

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:47.580
And I'm going to ask my son...hello,

00:46:47.581 --> 00:46:48.581
this

00:46:48.582 --> 00:46:50.094
one...can you show that one please?
That one? Yes. No, no, no, this one.

00:46:50.095 --> 00:46:50.551
There it is.

00:47:05.920 --> 00:47:06.920
Can you see that?

00:47:07.790 --> 00:47:10.419
Yeah, so that is a photograph

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:12.110
of a reserve police battalion 101.

00:47:13.660 --> 00:47:15.279
And all of you know, all of you in

00:47:15.280 --> 00:47:16.749
the field, know it from Christopher

00:47:16.750 --> 00:47:18.269
Browning's book Ordinary Men.

00:47:18.580 --> 00:47:19.569
This was the reserve police

00:47:19.570 --> 00:47:20.799
battalion that was at the center of

00:47:20.800 --> 00:47:21.699
Christopher Browning's

00:47:21.700 --> 00:47:22.700
investigation.

00:47:23.660 --> 00:47:25.449
And it's a classic book, Ordinary

00:47:25.450 --> 00:47:26.960
Men. And this is a photo I have...I

00:47:27.490 --> 00:47:29.379
published this photo in my article.

00:47:30.340 --> 00:47:31.959
It's a police reserve police

00:47:31.960 --> 00:47:33.716
battalion 101, after a

00:47:34.253 --> 00:47:35.889
ghetto roundup and deportation of

00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:37.780
some 1000 Jews

00:47:38.200 --> 00:47:39.200
in the ghetto Miedzyrzec Podlaski

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:43.990
in Poland.

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:45.140
They were deported to

00:47:46.090 --> 00:47:47.090
Majdanek on May 26th, 1943.

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:52.520
And it's a gleeful...in

00:47:53.650 --> 00:47:55.330
it you see the gleeful members

00:47:55.600 --> 00:47:57.437
of Reserve Police Battalion 101

00:47:58.240 --> 00:47:59.889
pointing to the spoils

00:48:00.070 --> 00:48:01.540
that they took from the Jewish

00:48:02.260 --> 00:48:04.600
victims that they had just deported

00:48:05.080 --> 00:48:06.080
to

00:48:07.300 --> 00:48:09.130
Majdanek. And, you know,

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:11.199
it's so hard to

00:48:11.230 --> 00:48:12.230
divine what the

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:15.129
motivation of

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:17.989
these men were.

00:48:18.260 --> 00:48:19.929
And Christopher Browning,

00:48:20.200 --> 00:48:21.560
in his most recent volume of...the

00:48:21.561 --> 00:48:23.100
Third Edition, revised edition, of

00:48:23.590 --> 00:48:25.090
his book Ordinary Men,

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:26.829
points to the

00:48:27.490 --> 00:48:28.479
fusion.

00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:29.679
When we tried to understand

00:48:29.800 --> 00:48:31.719
motivation, we should think

00:48:31.720 --> 00:48:33.430
about the synergy

00:48:33.910 --> 00:48:35.859
between situation

00:48:36.220 --> 00:48:37.449
and ideology.

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:39.570
And I agree with that, for

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:41.679
the most part. But I think what this

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:43.101
does also show...this picture

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:45.214
shows that, and

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:47.529
the other pictures that appear in

00:48:47.530 --> 00:48:48.530
the album, show what

00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:51.280
I just said, calling

00:48:51.370 --> 00:48:53.800
my article the creation

00:48:53.920 --> 00:48:55.929
of a permissive

00:48:55.930 --> 00:48:57.610
environment, a

00:48:58.030 --> 00:49:00.159
de facto open season

00:49:00.700 --> 00:49:01.827
on Poles, Poland's Jews

00:49:02.650 --> 00:49:03.650
in which men in this

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:06.849
battalion could torment,

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:09.010
deport and kill Jews

00:49:09.220 --> 00:49:10.329
with impunity.

00:49:10.360 --> 00:49:12.460
And I borrow a phrase from

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:14.328
my colleague and good friend, Alon

00:49:14.329 --> 00:49:15.329
Confino,

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:19.989
whose book is called A World Without

00:49:19.990 --> 00:49:21.159
Jews. It seems to me that the

00:49:21.160 --> 00:49:22.160
photographs that

00:49:23.320 --> 00:49:25.840
appear in this album from 1946

00:49:26.950 --> 00:49:29.200
display an attitude

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:31.059
among the Germans who took them

00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:32.548
that they could imagine a world

00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:35.600
without Jews.

00:49:35.630 --> 00:49:37.489
That's the argument that I make.

00:49:37.880 --> 00:49:39.432
But there's more to the album than

00:49:39.433 --> 00:49:40.433
just that.

00:49:40.628 --> 00:49:41.628
At the end of the

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:44.621
album, the editor, Gershon

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:46.729
Taffet, inserted a few photos

00:49:46.730 --> 00:49:49.170
taken by Jews that extoll

00:49:49.190 --> 00:49:50.480
Jewish resistance.

00:49:50.890 --> 00:49:52.789
And I argue that it's an attempt

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:55.329
to subvert the so-called German

00:49:55.330 --> 00:49:57.139
gaze in the album

00:49:57.350 --> 00:49:59.198
with the Jewish gaze.

00:49:59.540 --> 00:50:00.919
And I just want to show you one

00:50:00.920 --> 00:50:02.976
more...could you show?

00:50:04.400 --> 00:50:05.400
Thank you.

00:50:06.290 --> 00:50:07.290
Is it this one? Yes, thank you.

00:50:10.610 --> 00:50:11.610
Okay, one sec. Hold for tech.

00:50:12.730 --> 00:50:13.730
Hey, Gabi?

00:50:13.840 --> 00:50:15.131
Yes, I have...30

00:50:16.030 --> 00:50:17.030
seconds.

00:50:17.031 --> 00:50:17.283
Okay, great.

00:50:18.841 --> 00:50:19.841
Thank you.

00:50:20.420 --> 00:50:21.420
This one. Yes.

00:50:24.810 --> 00:50:25.829
OK. Does anyone see that?

00:50:26.110 --> 00:50:27.960
And that's a photo collage

00:50:28.770 --> 00:50:30.149
that was made in the Lodz ghetto in

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:31.238
1941 by a survivor who

00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:32.980
took the name in Israel, Arie
Ben-Menachem.

00:50:34.380 --> 00:50:35.939
It's called "Przwtrawamy." It means

00:50:35.940 --> 00:50:37.263
"we shall survive." So this

00:50:37.890 --> 00:50:39.269
is how the album ends.

00:50:39.510 --> 00:50:40.499
It ends on a hopeful note.

00:50:40.500 --> 00:50:41.758
But I just want to say that...I

00:50:42.930 --> 00:50:43.930
also am

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:48.420
a great fan of Larry's work, and

00:50:48.450 --> 00:50:49.920
I am, of course, familiar with

00:50:50.340 --> 00:50:52.469
his concept of unheroic

00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:54.010
memory.

00:50:54.240 --> 00:50:55.808
And I just want to say that even

00:50:56.010 --> 00:50:57.333
though it ends on a hopeful

00:50:57.840 --> 00:50:59.550
note, one,

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:01.710
the whole album, though,

00:51:02.070 --> 00:51:03.899
is permeated by

00:51:03.900 --> 00:51:05.639
this idea of

00:51:05.760 --> 00:51:07.139
unheroic memories.

00:51:07.230 --> 00:51:09.510
So there's this tension

00:51:10.020 --> 00:51:12.240
in the album between

00:51:12.480 --> 00:51:14.519
ending on a high note and

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:16.739
attestation of the horror

00:51:16.950 --> 00:51:18.629
that befell Polish Jews during the

00:51:18.630 --> 00:51:19.469
Holocaust.

00:51:19.470 --> 00:51:20.479
Thank you very much.

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:22.058
And I want to thank everyone for

00:51:22.320 --> 00:51:23.550
their contribution to

00:51:24.450 --> 00:51:25.450
the special issue.

00:51:27.290 --> 00:51:28.290
Thank you so much, Gabi.

00:51:30.290 --> 00:51:31.290
Good.

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:33.950
Well, that's all the contributors.

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:36.140
I just wanted to add

00:51:36.440 --> 00:51:37.861
a little personal note, which

00:51:38.420 --> 00:51:39.679
is that like many others,

00:51:40.250 --> 00:51:42.019
I met Larry

00:51:42.110 --> 00:51:43.790
first, for the first time when I was

00:51:43.880 --> 00:51:46.130
a postdoc in the mid 90s,

00:51:46.550 --> 00:51:48.289
was also rather intimidated

00:51:49.130 --> 00:51:51.020
because, of course, you know, he was

00:51:51.110 --> 00:51:52.110
Lawrence Langer.

00:51:52.970 --> 00:51:54.949
And it wasn't so many years since

00:51:55.710 --> 00:51:56.710
I read

00:51:57.740 --> 00:51:59.719
the Holocaust testimonies book and

00:51:59.780 --> 00:52:01.699
was kind of overwhelmed.

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:03.090
And Larry paid

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:05.619
me the great honor of taking me

00:52:05.620 --> 00:52:07.359
seriously and treating me

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:08.821
like, like a peer.

00:52:09.220 --> 00:52:11.469
And he read parts of my doctorate.

00:52:11.740 --> 00:52:12.790
And we talked about it

00:52:14.050 --> 00:52:16.210
and made me feel as though

00:52:17.770 --> 00:52:19.150
I was

00:52:19.900 --> 00:52:21.566
an equal and had a contribution to

00:52:21.730 --> 00:52:23.319
make. And it was really wonderful,

00:52:23.320 --> 00:52:25.359
was extremely important for me

00:52:26.350 --> 00:52:28.090
at that point in my career to

00:52:28.270 --> 00:52:30.249
be treated like that by

00:52:30.250 --> 00:52:32.050
somebody like Lawrence Langer.

00:52:32.110 --> 00:52:34.179
So my own personal thanks

00:52:34.180 --> 00:52:35.503
as well to add to, to those

00:52:36.130 --> 00:52:37.389
of everybody else's.

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:38.480
And on that, I

00:52:39.370 --> 00:52:41.349
would like now to invite

00:52:42.040 --> 00:52:43.750
Larry to respond, to

00:52:44.440 --> 00:52:45.440
say a few words. Whatever, whatever

00:52:46.750 --> 00:52:48.070
you like. Over to you.

00:52:51.890 --> 00:52:53.840
Well, can you hear me?

00:52:56.150 --> 00:52:57.150
We can hear you.

00:53:01.260 --> 00:53:02.270
You can hear me, all right.

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:03.917
Well have about 100 things to say

00:53:04.010 --> 00:53:05.676
and only time to say about five of

00:53:05.760 --> 00:53:07.570
them. Before I forget, Gabi,

00:53:09.190 --> 00:53:10.758
it's very nice to have a 20 year

00:53:11.060 --> 00:53:13.249
old son to help you with things.

00:53:13.610 --> 00:53:15.599
My friend, it's even nicer to have

00:53:15.600 --> 00:53:16.923
his 63 year old son, who is

00:53:17.510 --> 00:53:19.489
a grandfather, to help

00:53:19.490 --> 00:53:20.419
you with things.

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:21.420
So be prepared.

00:53:21.440 --> 00:53:22.879
You have a great future ahead of

00:53:22.880 --> 00:53:24.859
you. My son is my right arm

00:53:25.130 --> 00:53:26.130
and my left arm.

00:53:27.560 --> 00:53:29.599
Unfortunately, I wish you hadn't

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:30.600
said one thing

00:53:31.820 --> 00:53:33.829
and it had to be echoed by

00:53:33.830 --> 00:53:36.019
Dan. My entire

00:53:36.020 --> 00:53:37.392
adult life, my wife has been

00:53:37.880 --> 00:53:39.448
asking me, why do you intimidate

00:53:40.130 --> 00:53:41.210
people so much?

00:53:41.900 --> 00:53:43.829
And I tell her, me, you know,

00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:45.559
the nicest guy in the world, I never

00:53:45.560 --> 00:53:47.179
intimidate anyone.

00:53:48.050 --> 00:53:50.119
The minute this webinar is over,

00:53:50.720 --> 00:53:52.489
my wife is going to charge into my

00:53:52.490 --> 00:53:53.826
arms. There, you see, you see, not

00:53:55.220 --> 00:53:56.369
only did you intimidate Gabi, you

00:53:56.370 --> 00:53:57.370
intimidated Dan,

00:53:58.460 --> 00:53:59.540
you know, own up to it.

00:53:59.720 --> 00:54:00.920
Well, it's still not true.

00:54:01.820 --> 00:54:02.820
Now,

00:54:03.650 --> 00:54:05.719
my young friends, let me tell

00:54:05.720 --> 00:54:06.720
you something.

00:54:07.790 --> 00:54:09.889
I think you

00:54:09.890 --> 00:54:11.360
made the mistake of saying,

00:54:11.720 --> 00:54:13.546
well, the festschrift is

00:54:13.550 --> 00:54:14.550
a little late.

00:54:14.720 --> 00:54:16.324
Does a year...what's in a year?

00:54:19.060 --> 00:54:19.839
Be prepared.

00:54:19.840 --> 00:54:21.457
Be prepared when you reach my age

00:54:21.516 --> 00:54:22.643
there's a lot in a year.

00:54:23.350 --> 00:54:25.016
Now in writing my essay, which I'm

00:54:25.230 --> 00:54:26.758
not going to talk about...my life.

00:54:28.917 --> 00:54:30.339
I was reminded of something that I

00:54:30.340 --> 00:54:31.690
hadn't thought about before.

00:54:32.170 --> 00:54:35.050
When I visited Dachau in 1955,

00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:37.630
Raul Hilberg was a graduate student.

00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:41.679
Christopher Browning was in

00:54:41.680 --> 00:54:42.730
junior high school.

00:54:43.330 --> 00:54:45.159
And this blows my mind

00:54:45.640 --> 00:54:47.630
more than anything, my...late

00:54:48.280 --> 00:54:49.510
in life dear friend

00:54:50.439 --> 00:54:51.460
Wendy Lower was not even born.

00:54:52.840 --> 00:54:54.670
And so I've had to deal with

00:54:54.850 --> 00:54:56.530
this issue with the passage of time.

00:54:57.050 --> 00:54:58.599
Now, there

00:54:59.210 --> 00:55:00.533
is no point in my repeating

00:55:01.030 --> 00:55:02.949
the thanks to everyone, you again

00:55:02.980 --> 00:55:04.389
and again. Thank you, everyone.

00:55:04.780 --> 00:55:06.249
I just want to mention, I'm sure I

00:55:06.250 --> 00:55:08.199
have no idea how much

00:55:08.200 --> 00:55:10.129
time and energy Dan

00:55:10.150 --> 00:55:12.460
and Dawn and Gabi must've

00:55:12.790 --> 00:55:14.349
put into preparing

00:55:14.680 --> 00:55:15.680
this volume.

00:55:16.870 --> 00:55:18.669
I would like to add one thanks which

00:55:18.790 --> 00:55:20.379
no one would mention because they

00:55:20.380 --> 00:55:21.550
didn't know what to mention.

00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:23.560
The cover of this

00:55:24.130 --> 00:55:25.449
print, the in print

00:55:27.400 --> 00:55:28.968
festschrift is a painting by the

00:55:29.350 --> 00:55:31.719
child survivor of the Vilna ghetto,

00:55:31.990 --> 00:55:34.059
Samuel Bak, called the Creation

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:35.289
of Adam.

00:55:35.290 --> 00:55:36.729
And I would like to thank Bernie

00:55:36.730 --> 00:55:38.249
Pucker of the Pucker gallery in

00:55:38.380 --> 00:55:40.659
Boston for given permission

00:55:40.660 --> 00:55:41.829
to use that painting.

00:55:42.170 --> 00:55:43.719
And I hope those of you who will

00:55:43.720 --> 00:55:44.720
be...who

00:55:45.790 --> 00:55:47.619
will be getting a copy

00:55:47.620 --> 00:55:49.237
of the print version will look at

00:55:49.360 --> 00:55:51.219
that painting because

00:55:51.250 --> 00:55:53.590
it's Sam Bak's post Holocaust

00:55:53.620 --> 00:55:54.620
version of Michaelangelo's

00:55:55.600 --> 00:55:56.600
famous

00:55:58.270 --> 00:55:59.936
version of the creation of Adam on

00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:02.349
the ceiling of Sistine Chapel.

00:56:02.860 --> 00:56:03.860
And when you look at the Adam,

00:56:04.720 --> 00:56:06.340
you have to ask the question

00:56:06.610 --> 00:56:07.753
that Primo Levi

00:56:08.920 --> 00:56:10.929
asked, is this a man?

00:56:11.400 --> 00:56:12.639
And not only that, when you look at

00:56:12.640 --> 00:56:13.840
the image of God

00:56:14.530 --> 00:56:16.119
in that painting, you have to ask

00:56:16.120 --> 00:56:18.399
the question...to ask less often,

00:56:19.150 --> 00:56:20.489
is this a God?

00:56:20.860 --> 00:56:22.630
So that painting really

00:56:22.960 --> 00:56:24.639
creates a kind of introduction

00:56:25.210 --> 00:56:26.260
to that volume.

00:56:26.620 --> 00:56:27.958
Now, I read each of these essays

00:56:31.110 --> 00:56:32.699
twice and

00:56:33.450 --> 00:56:35.039
was extraordinary. The second time

00:56:35.040 --> 00:56:36.040
through,

00:56:38.010 --> 00:56:39.020
I realized

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:41.429
things I didn't read the first time

00:56:41.430 --> 00:56:43.380
through...that these are

00:56:43.440 --> 00:56:45.389
such serious

00:56:45.870 --> 00:56:47.438
investigations of such a variety

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:49.469
of issues.

00:56:49.980 --> 00:56:52.139
And I just offer my heartfelt thanks

00:56:52.140 --> 00:56:53.610
to all of you for contributing

00:56:54.300 --> 00:56:55.440
to this volume.

00:56:55.740 --> 00:56:57.209
One of the things I noticed, and I

00:56:57.210 --> 00:56:58.649
don't have time to talk about it,

00:56:59.280 --> 00:57:00.449
is that there is a kind of

00:57:00.450 --> 00:57:02.369
interaction, unintended, of course,

00:57:02.400 --> 00:57:03.899
because you haven't read each

00:57:03.900 --> 00:57:05.130
other's contributions,

00:57:05.770 --> 00:57:07.679
the relationship

00:57:07.680 --> 00:57:09.360
between some of these essays.

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:11.489
Just imagine, Dawn

00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:13.919
and Gabi, if

00:57:15.460 --> 00:57:17.309
Jan Karski had been

00:57:17.310 --> 00:57:19.169
able to walk into the office

00:57:20.250 --> 00:57:21.900
of President Roosevelt's

00:57:22.410 --> 00:57:24.300
carrying Gabi's volume

00:57:25.860 --> 00:57:27.149
under his arm and trying to get

00:57:27.150 --> 00:57:28.919
President Roosevelt to understand

00:57:28.940 --> 00:57:31.260
what he's trying to tell him when

00:57:31.560 --> 00:57:33.540
he went in and looked at

00:57:33.870 --> 00:57:35.242
the condition of the Jews in

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:36.941
the Warsaw ghetto.

00:57:38.370 --> 00:57:39.869
I want you to think about one thing,

00:57:40.410 --> 00:57:41.978
neither in your essay or in your

00:57:42.360 --> 00:57:44.219
presentation now have you shown us

00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:45.929
a picture of one of these atrocity

00:57:45.930 --> 00:57:46.930
photographs.

00:57:47.460 --> 00:57:49.290
And I'd like you to think about why.

00:57:50.520 --> 00:57:51.869
Because we need to see them.

00:57:52.410 --> 00:57:53.887
And I hope you will in an e-mail

00:57:53.888 --> 00:57:55.679
tell me, how can I get a copy of

00:57:55.680 --> 00:57:57.059
this? None of the libraries that I

00:57:57.060 --> 00:57:58.229
checked, they have a copy.

00:57:58.710 --> 00:57:59.952
I'm the reason we have them here.

00:58:00.600 --> 00:58:01.600
Now,

00:58:04.850 --> 00:58:06.679
what I appreciate most about

00:58:06.680 --> 00:58:08.052
these essays is that instead

00:58:08.780 --> 00:58:10.152
of answering questions, they

00:58:10.610 --> 00:58:12.589
raised questions, all kinds

00:58:12.590 --> 00:58:14.749
of questions that have no answers

00:58:15.590 --> 00:58:16.590
and even

00:58:17.570 --> 00:58:18.570
may be unanswerable.

00:58:21.550 --> 00:58:23.539
And I want to show

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:24.840
two examples at the essays...Jennifer

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:31.160
and her wonderful essay on

00:58:31.170 --> 00:58:33.090
Primo Levi writes

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:34.120
the following, just

00:58:34.980 --> 00:58:37.139
one sentence, "considering

00:58:37.170 --> 00:58:39.150
if something is so,

00:58:39.570 --> 00:58:41.519
is not a questioning that

00:58:41.520 --> 00:58:42.840
produces an answer.

00:58:43.830 --> 00:58:45.689
Rather, it is a dwelling with a

00:58:45.690 --> 00:58:47.909
painful reality that cannot

00:58:47.910 --> 00:58:48.910
be resolved.

00:58:49.200 --> 00:58:50.364
And brackets judgment, requiring

00:58:51.600 --> 00:58:53.849
our continued attention, time

00:58:54.080 --> 00:58:55.919
and ongoing reflection,

00:58:56.400 --> 00:58:58.017
even though we can't go, can't be

00:58:58.320 --> 00:59:00.035
resolved." I think it's, it's a gem

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:01.340
of a sentence, Jennifer.

00:59:01.980 --> 00:59:03.480
And thank you

00:59:03.930 --> 00:59:04.930
for it.

00:59:05.190 --> 00:59:06.190
The other really

00:59:08.680 --> 00:59:11.050
moment that concentrates

00:59:11.080 --> 00:59:12.600
in a sentence, is in

00:59:13.570 --> 00:59:14.570
Sara's essay...and

00:59:15.640 --> 00:59:16.929
Sara, thank you so much.

00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:18.675
I never heard of Sarah Kofman in my

00:59:18.700 --> 00:59:19.729
life before where you

00:59:20.650 --> 00:59:21.650
mentioned her.

00:59:21.820 --> 00:59:23.679
And for some reason, my local

00:59:23.680 --> 00:59:25.449
library had a copy of her book,

00:59:26.290 --> 00:59:27.876
which I took out and thank God it

00:59:29.290 --> 00:59:31.150
was only 80 pages, so I read it.

00:59:31.390 --> 00:59:32.811
I'm currently reading the new

00:59:32.860 --> 00:59:33.860
biography

00:59:34.720 --> 00:59:35.720
by Volker Ullrich of Adolf

00:59:36.940 --> 00:59:38.889
Hitler. Each one weighs about 10

00:59:38.890 --> 00:59:39.890
pounds.

00:59:40.540 --> 00:59:42.550
Each one is about 800 pages

00:59:42.820 --> 00:59:44.800
long. And just imagine, I mean,

00:59:45.100 --> 00:59:46.344
Hitler came to power once, it's

00:59:47.070 --> 00:59:48.389
eighty three years ago.

00:59:48.790 --> 00:59:49.921
And in 2020, this year volume

00:59:50.770 --> 00:59:52.659
two came out full of

00:59:52.660 --> 00:59:54.429
information that

00:59:55.030 --> 00:59:57.250
hadn't been used before.

00:59:57.730 --> 00:59:59.540
So this subject is not

00:59:59.560 --> 01:00:01.300
dead and will never die.

01:00:01.530 --> 01:00:03.039
Let me read you just that

01:00:03.430 --> 01:00:05.739
other sentence from Sarah Kofman's

01:00:05.950 --> 01:00:07.764
book: "Because he was a Jew, my

01:00:08.890 --> 01:00:10.869
father died in Auschwitz.

01:00:11.770 --> 01:00:13.119
How can it not be said

01:00:14.080 --> 01:00:15.369
and how can it be said?

01:00:16.090 --> 01:00:17.658
How can one speak of that before

01:00:17.902 --> 01:00:19.989
which all possibility of speech

01:00:20.360 --> 01:00:22.209
ceases?" Now,

01:00:23.590 --> 01:00:24.790
behind that line,

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:28.599
there exist questions

01:00:30.160 --> 01:00:32.290
which will never have an answer,

01:00:33.130 --> 01:00:34.698
because she found out later what

01:00:34.900 --> 01:00:36.459
happened to her father, her father,

01:00:36.730 --> 01:00:38.679
a rabbi, an Orthodox Jew

01:00:39.230 --> 01:00:41.320
who refused to work

01:00:41.350 --> 01:00:42.350
on the Sabbath.

01:00:43.030 --> 01:00:44.780
So as a punishment, the

01:00:44.850 --> 01:00:45.850
Germans buried him

01:00:46.750 --> 01:00:47.750
alive.

01:00:49.060 --> 01:00:50.849
And I asked myself, these are the

01:00:50.850 --> 01:00:51.850
questions that rise. Who

01:00:52.830 --> 01:00:53.830
gave that order? Who

01:00:54.940 --> 01:00:55.940
dug that hole?

01:00:58.320 --> 01:01:00.389
What were the onlookers thinking

01:01:00.390 --> 01:01:01.619
while it was happening?

01:01:02.700 --> 01:01:03.700
And what was her

01:01:04.690 --> 01:01:06.059
rabbi father thinking

01:01:06.720 --> 01:01:08.043
as the dirt piled up around

01:01:08.820 --> 01:01:10.409
him? These are questions raised by

01:01:10.410 --> 01:01:12.329
the Holocaust for which we don't

01:01:12.330 --> 01:01:13.555
have the answers, but for

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:15.892
which we are obliged, about

01:01:17.340 --> 01:01:18.449
which we're about,

01:01:19.350 --> 01:01:21.210
we are obliged to think.

01:01:22.320 --> 01:01:23.909
I have a lot of reflections on that.

01:01:23.960 --> 01:01:25.529
But, you know, we've gone more than

01:01:25.530 --> 01:01:27.329
an hour. I just want to say one more

01:01:27.330 --> 01:01:28.770
thing and then I'm going to stop

01:01:28.800 --> 01:01:30.510
because there may be questions and

01:01:30.810 --> 01:01:32.729
we need to allow time for

01:01:32.730 --> 01:01:33.730
that.

01:01:33.960 --> 01:01:35.789
I watched a interview many

01:01:35.790 --> 01:01:37.409
years ago, an interview

01:01:37.950 --> 01:01:39.581
at the Fortunoff Archives

01:01:40.320 --> 01:01:42.030
from a man who told his story.

01:01:42.450 --> 01:01:44.159
At the very end, the interviewer

01:01:44.460 --> 01:01:46.829
made what I consider to be a mistake

01:01:46.830 --> 01:01:49.169
of asking him, do you have any final

01:01:49.170 --> 01:01:50.395
comments that you like to

01:01:51.030 --> 01:01:52.030
offer us?

01:01:52.590 --> 01:01:54.305
That is usually an invitation for a

01:01:54.410 --> 01:01:55.831
little moral homily about the

01:01:56.280 --> 01:01:57.280
need to, the

01:01:59.260 --> 01:02:00.485
need to be good to people

01:02:01.390 --> 01:02:03.279
and be kind and not to bully and so

01:02:03.280 --> 01:02:04.280
forth.

01:02:04.420 --> 01:02:05.420
That's not what he did.

01:02:06.130 --> 01:02:07.840
He said...all my life, he says,

01:02:08.440 --> 01:02:09.579
I've been haunted by

01:02:10.310 --> 01:02:11.310
a question

01:02:13.900 --> 01:02:14.900
and this is the mode in which I like
to end.

01:02:15.040 --> 01:02:16.045
What's the question?

01:02:17.290 --> 01:02:18.290
He said, what

01:02:19.970 --> 01:02:21.050
happened to my mother?

01:02:23.430 --> 01:02:24.430
When they killed her.

01:02:25.720 --> 01:02:26.720
Was she alone? Was she with other
people? Was

01:02:29.660 --> 01:02:30.660
she afraid?

01:02:31.400 --> 01:02:32.400
What was she thinking?

01:02:33.700 --> 01:02:35.464
I'll let you know, and he said, I'll

01:02:35.590 --> 01:02:36.590
never know.

01:02:37.740 --> 01:02:39.560
So an important dimension

01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:41.125
of Holocaust studies, and

01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:43.079
it happened in so many of these

01:02:43.080 --> 01:02:44.760
essays is

01:02:45.060 --> 01:02:46.590
exposing questions

01:02:47.130 --> 01:02:48.152
for which there is no answer and

01:02:49.770 --> 01:02:52.229
for which we are nonetheless obliged

01:02:53.040 --> 01:02:54.040
to continue, continue

01:02:55.110 --> 01:02:56.110
searching for one.

01:02:56.850 --> 01:02:58.670
So I hope you young

01:02:59.010 --> 01:03:00.420
people, and I say that

01:03:00.920 --> 01:03:02.270
from my position as someone who

01:03:03.470 --> 01:03:05.129
is now going on 92, to carry on with

01:03:05.130 --> 01:03:06.130
this kind of work.

01:03:07.050 --> 01:03:08.275
It's almost inconceivable

01:03:09.300 --> 01:03:11.250
that we're approaching a century

01:03:12.030 --> 01:03:13.647
from the time when Hitler came to

01:03:13.980 --> 01:03:14.980
power.

01:03:15.060 --> 01:03:16.919
And essays like these are still

01:03:16.920 --> 01:03:17.920
being written.

01:03:18.390 --> 01:03:20.519
And I hope they will continue

01:03:20.520 --> 01:03:21.520
to be for

01:03:22.440 --> 01:03:23.440
a long, long time.

01:03:24.370 --> 01:03:26.329
I mean, I bless you all, paying

01:03:26.370 --> 01:03:27.900
such attention to what I have done.

01:03:28.350 --> 01:03:30.059
You can't imagine how good

01:03:30.180 --> 01:03:31.679
this all makes me feel.

01:03:32.160 --> 01:03:33.960
And being friends

01:03:34.560 --> 01:03:36.689
and not people

01:03:36.690 --> 01:03:38.356
I'm intimidated, but being friends

01:03:38.890 --> 01:03:40.769
have meant so much to me.

01:03:40.820 --> 01:03:42.570
And just looking at you

01:03:44.010 --> 01:03:45.189
fills my heart with warmth. So thank

01:03:45.190 --> 01:03:45.979
you, thank you.

01:03:45.980 --> 01:03:46.980
Thank you.

01:03:49.540 --> 01:03:50.540
Thank you, Larry.

01:03:51.550 --> 01:03:52.922
It's an enormous honor and a

01:03:53.140 --> 01:03:54.588
pleasure for all of us, I think, to,

01:03:54.589 --> 01:03:55.667
to hear your words and

01:03:56.440 --> 01:03:57.670
we're not intimidated anymore.

01:03:58.450 --> 01:03:59.871
We were just, just reflecting

01:04:00.580 --> 01:04:01.630
on on the early days.

01:04:03.070 --> 01:04:04.239
It's really wonderful to hear what

01:04:04.330 --> 01:04:05.330
you say.

01:04:05.550 --> 01:04:06.902
I think I'm speaking for myself,

01:04:06.903 --> 01:04:07.903
what...what you say

01:04:08.980 --> 01:04:10.058
about asking questions

01:04:11.170 --> 01:04:12.787
that cannot be answered is really

01:04:12.820 --> 01:04:14.290
goes to the heart of things, I

01:04:14.560 --> 01:04:15.699
think, and particularly

01:04:16.750 --> 01:04:18.489
for historians who like

01:04:18.790 --> 01:04:19.790
to answer questions.

01:04:19.884 --> 01:04:21.820
In this

01:04:22.270 --> 01:04:23.270
subject, extremely

01:04:24.310 --> 01:04:25.310
important to remember,

01:04:28.110 --> 01:04:29.819
I think what you've taught us, which

01:04:29.820 --> 01:04:31.290
is that there is no redemptive

01:04:31.710 --> 01:04:32.710
message to be gained

01:04:34.170 --> 01:04:35.610
from studying the Holocaust.

01:04:36.610 --> 01:04:38.250
And there are no final answers

01:04:39.330 --> 01:04:41.249
in history or in

01:04:41.250 --> 01:04:42.269
any other respect.

01:04:42.380 --> 01:04:43.829
And I think that's an enormous

01:04:43.830 --> 01:04:45.689
contribution and continues to be

01:04:45.690 --> 01:04:47.939
something that has to be repeated

01:04:48.300 --> 01:04:49.739
over and over again, particularly

01:04:49.740 --> 01:04:50.740
given the state of

01:04:51.690 --> 01:04:53.329
public engagement with the Holocaust

01:04:54.240 --> 01:04:56.530
and some of the immense

01:04:57.450 --> 01:04:58.773
analyzation that take place

01:04:59.340 --> 01:05:00.299
on a daily basis.

01:05:00.300 --> 01:05:02.429
So we're all immensely

01:05:02.430 --> 01:05:04.050
grateful to you for your work.

01:05:05.190 --> 01:05:07.229
And I will also

01:05:07.230 --> 01:05:08.520
finally add here a little

01:05:09.120 --> 01:05:10.786
advertisement, which is that Larry

01:05:10.920 --> 01:05:12.300
was too modest to mention that

01:05:13.080 --> 01:05:14.670
another collection of his own essays

01:05:15.420 --> 01:05:17.399
will be published in

01:05:17.970 --> 01:05:20.190
the New Year with Palgrave.

01:05:20.580 --> 01:05:22.500
So we have more of Larry's work

01:05:23.070 --> 01:05:24.539
still to look forward to.

01:05:25.380 --> 01:05:26.580
It's called the...it's the "After

01:05:26.581 --> 01:05:28.360
Death of the Holocaust" is the

01:05:28.370 --> 01:05:29.370
title.

01:05:30.030 --> 01:05:31.030
After Death of the
Holocaust...coming, coming

01:05:31.320 --> 01:05:32.320
to a book...

01:05:32.321 --> 01:05:32.510
February.

01:05:34.263 --> 01:05:35.169
Thank you, Larry. Stephen, do we

01:05:35.170 --> 01:05:36.199
have time to take a question?

01:05:37.120 --> 01:05:38.120
Of course.

01:05:38.730 --> 01:05:40.659
There's actually one here, Larry,

01:05:40.660 --> 01:05:42.579
that you might like to respond

01:05:42.580 --> 01:05:44.459
to, which I don't

01:05:44.470 --> 01:05:45.760
know, we don't have a name.

01:05:45.790 --> 01:05:47.440
An anonymous attendee says

01:05:48.690 --> 01:05:50.500
the great presence of some art,

01:05:50.530 --> 01:05:51.530
for example, the art of

01:05:53.140 --> 01:05:54.249
Samuel Bak in your writing since

01:05:54.320 --> 01:05:55.809
1995, please comment.

01:05:55.930 --> 01:05:57.349
And you already have, obviously, on

01:05:57.350 --> 01:05:59.169
the painting, which adorns

01:05:59.200 --> 01:06:00.489
the cover of the journal.

01:06:00.520 --> 01:06:01.449
But I wonder if you'd like to say

01:06:01.450 --> 01:06:02.295
anymore about, about Samuel Bak's

01:06:02.296 --> 01:06:03.296
work.

01:06:04.830 --> 01:06:05.830
Yes,

01:06:06.870 --> 01:06:08.459
I came late in life to the work

01:06:09.240 --> 01:06:11.719
of Sam Bak. We have now done eleven

01:06:11.730 --> 01:06:13.019
volumes together.

01:06:13.470 --> 01:06:14.891
He does a series of paintings

01:06:15.360 --> 01:06:17.369
and I write a long essay.

01:06:17.760 --> 01:06:19.634
And Bernie Pucker, through his

01:06:20.370 --> 01:06:21.546
wizardry, managed to get

01:06:22.260 --> 01:06:24.019
the volumes published

01:06:24.280 --> 01:06:25.280
right now.

01:06:25.310 --> 01:06:27.119
And I'll just say two or three

01:06:27.120 --> 01:06:28.649
sentences about Sam, because this is

01:06:28.650 --> 01:06:29.940
not really about Sam.

01:06:30.340 --> 01:06:31.590
But along with

01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:34.323
Felix Nussbaum, they for me

01:06:34.860 --> 01:06:36.239
are the two great artists of the

01:06:36.240 --> 01:06:37.240
Holocaust.

01:06:37.950 --> 01:06:40.139
But Sam tries

01:06:40.140 --> 01:06:41.610
to present visually

01:06:42.150 --> 01:06:44.070
the way the Holocaust has fractured

01:06:44.330 --> 01:06:46.230
our world in the years

01:06:46.260 --> 01:06:47.260
after the war.

01:06:48.420 --> 01:06:50.429
It's a unique contribution

01:06:50.430 --> 01:06:51.430
to the world of art.

01:06:52.020 --> 01:06:53.460
He should be better known.

01:06:53.490 --> 01:06:55.409
But I mean, people tell me

01:06:55.410 --> 01:06:56.410
again and again, I

01:06:57.270 --> 01:06:58.859
can't hang a painting like that in

01:06:58.860 --> 01:07:00.239
my living room because I have to

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:01.240
look at it.

01:07:01.560 --> 01:07:02.669
Well, it's like looking at the

01:07:02.670 --> 01:07:03.989
photographs in

01:07:04.770 --> 01:07:05.770
these volume.

01:07:06.300 --> 01:07:07.649
We can't look at it but we have to

01:07:07.650 --> 01:07:08.650
look at it.

01:07:09.060 --> 01:07:11.130
But if you're interested in pursuing

01:07:11.610 --> 01:07:13.169
the work of Sam Bak and it's

01:07:13.170 --> 01:07:14.670
certainly worth pursuing, just

01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:16.000
go onto

01:07:18.240 --> 01:07:19.240
Puckergallery.com, go into artists,
go

01:07:19.260 --> 01:07:20.260
into Samuel Bak.

01:07:20.700 --> 01:07:22.260
You'll be able to see a lot of his

01:07:22.270 --> 01:07:23.270
paintings, they're really worth

01:07:23.670 --> 01:07:24.670
investigating.

01:07:27.460 --> 01:07:28.600
Thank you, Larry.

01:07:29.710 --> 01:07:31.270
There's a comment here from

01:07:31.810 --> 01:07:33.849
Wendy Lauer, it's nice

01:07:33.850 --> 01:07:34.850
that Wendy's here.

01:07:35.310 --> 01:07:37.210
And Wendy

01:07:38.320 --> 01:07:39.320
says

01:07:41.020 --> 01:07:42.609
she wants to pay tribute to, to your

01:07:42.610 --> 01:07:43.610
work and cites

01:07:44.800 --> 01:07:46.659
one of the, one particular

01:07:46.660 --> 01:07:48.039
sentence that you've, that you've

01:07:48.040 --> 01:07:48.849
written.

01:07:48.850 --> 01:07:50.679
"When human beings cease

01:07:50.680 --> 01:07:52.052
to be emissaries or legatees

01:07:52.570 --> 01:07:54.760
of love and become instead agents

01:07:54.820 --> 01:07:56.143
or victims of power on such

01:07:56.770 --> 01:07:58.659
a massive scale, we may have

01:07:58.660 --> 01:08:00.909
witnessed a shift in civilization's

01:08:00.910 --> 01:08:02.710
priorities with whose

01:08:02.860 --> 01:08:04.477
psychological bequest we continue

01:08:05.020 --> 01:08:06.020
to struggle."Would

01:08:08.380 --> 01:08:09.520
you like to comment or

01:08:10.420 --> 01:08:11.420
leave it as it stands?

01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:14.622
Ask me November 4th. Alright, and
I'll comment then.

01:08:14.623 --> 01:08:16.156
Alright, I understand. I understand.
In that case, Stephen, do we want to

01:08:19.895 --> 01:08:20.895
trickle things to a close?

01:08:27.120 --> 01:08:28.820
I think so. There was one question,

01:08:28.950 --> 01:08:30.539
one other question about how to

01:08:30.899 --> 01:08:32.250
obtain a copy of the journal.

01:08:32.270 --> 01:08:33.509
I don't know if you want to just say

01:08:33.510 --> 01:08:35.279
a few words about

01:08:35.760 --> 01:08:37.560
how to get a copy of the journal.

01:08:40.080 --> 01:08:41.648
Well, for...of course, for those

01:08:41.700 --> 01:08:43.949
with institutional subscriptions

01:08:43.970 --> 01:08:44.999
to Taylor and Francis

01:08:46.020 --> 01:08:47.392
journals, it can be obtained

01:08:48.060 --> 01:08:50.369
through your institutional library.

01:08:50.399 --> 01:08:52.199
I think for those who, who don't

01:08:52.200 --> 01:08:54.210
have access to a university

01:08:54.930 --> 01:08:56.999
library, perhaps

01:08:57.000 --> 01:08:58.000
if you go to the...maybe Michal can

01:08:59.250 --> 01:09:00.478
comment. But perhaps if you go to

01:09:00.479 --> 01:09:01.889
the website of the Journal of

01:09:01.890 --> 01:09:03.509
Holocaust Research, you'll be able

01:09:03.510 --> 01:09:05.939
to see what access options

01:09:06.870 --> 01:09:07.870
there are there.

01:09:08.160 --> 01:09:09.889
But perhaps you could also email

01:09:10.350 --> 01:09:12.240
Michal Aharony and

01:09:12.300 --> 01:09:13.529
ask her about that as well.

01:09:14.850 --> 01:09:16.228
Otherwise, I think that's, that's

01:09:16.229 --> 01:09:17.009
all the questions.

01:09:17.010 --> 01:09:18.010
So it just falls

01:09:18.960 --> 01:09:20.038
to me to, to say thank

01:09:20.819 --> 01:09:21.629
you to the contributors.

01:09:21.630 --> 01:09:23.119
Again, thank you to Stephen and to

01:09:23.220 --> 01:09:24.626
Fortunoff Archive.

01:09:25.020 --> 01:09:26.999
Thank you to Michal and the

01:09:27.660 --> 01:09:28.890
Journal of Holocaust Research.

01:09:29.160 --> 01:09:30.689
And most of all, thank you to Larry.

01:09:31.520 --> 01:09:33.209
We're deeply

01:09:33.990 --> 01:09:35.509
honored to be able to celebrate

01:09:36.060 --> 01:09:37.060
your work in this way.

01:09:37.620 --> 01:09:39.420
Wish you a happy birthday

01:09:39.770 --> 01:09:40.949
and many more to come.

01:09:41.520 --> 01:09:41.909
Thank you.

